\p{
    I am an MPhil student in \em{Advanced Computer Science} and a Research Assistant at the \ucam, where I work on reasoning verification and verifier-guided post-training for large language models. 
    My current research studies how verification objectives and model architectures affect both verification performance and the representations learned by language models.
}

\p{
    Prior to Cambridge, I was a Research Assistant at \shailab, where I worked on reinforcement-learning methods that connect LLM reasoning with formal verification. I completed my undergraduate degree in \em{Computer Science} at \kcl with First Class Honours and an alumni of the Google DeepMind Education Programme cohort.
}


\p{
    My research interests lie in \strong{Natural Language Processing} and the \strong{post-training of Large Language Models}, particularly in how model behaviours can be specified, verified, and improved. 
    My current work focuses on reasoning verification, while I am increasingly interested in extending verification beyond settings with explicit notions of correctness to open-ended interactions, where properties such as behavioural fidelity must instead be grounded in natural-language specifications or protocols.
}

\p{
    More broadly, I aim to study how verification signals can be used both to evaluate and improve language models through post-training and reinforcement learning.
    I am also interested in mechanistic analyses of verifiers, particularly whether their judgments reflect the intended notion of validity or fidelity rather than exploitable shortcuts.
}

